        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 4,
        "titulo": "Informal «ты» or formal «вы»?",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: In English you say «you» to almost everybody. In Russian there are two forms, and choosing the right one shows respect. STEP 2: Use «ты» (ty) with friends, family, young colleagues and children. With «ты», say «Привет!» (pree-VYET) and «Пока!» (pah-KAH). STEP 3: Use «вы» (vy) with strangers, bosses, teachers, older people, and in shops and offices. With «вы», say «Здравствуйте!» (ZDRAH-stvooy-tyeh) and «До свидания!» (dah svee-DAH-nee-yah). Tip: the first «в» in «Здравствуйте» is not pronounced. STEP 4: «вы» is also the plural: if you speak to two people, even friends, you say «вы». STEP 5: When in doubt, use «вы». Nobody is offended by too much respect, and the other person can suggest «ты». STEP 6: Bosses and older people are usually addressed by first name and patronymic, the father's name: «Ирина Петровна» (ee-REE-nah pee-TROV-nah).",
        "comparacao": "English has something similar: «Hey» for a friend and «Sir» or «Madam» for someone who deserves respect. The difference is that, in Russian, the choice is mandatory in every sentence and it changes the greeting.",
        "exemplos": [
          {
            "ru": "Привет!",
            "pt": "Hi! For a friend."
          },
          {
            "ru": "Здравствуйте!",
            "pt": "Hello! For a stranger, the boss, the teacher."
          },
          {
            "ru": "Как тебя зовут?",
